{"author":{"id":"609bf866d4a5d716c19faf5d617b3bf3de478230d29602d72aad5aa90a80466f"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1794316845,"nonce":"bvNxKsqvXKjpSgH+7Yf6JOrV9pM=","message":"Vertical slice (crate ash_wasm_emit, no LLVM, builds for wasm32-unknown-unknown): numeric subset of AIR -\u003e wasm (bool/i32/i64/f32/f64 values; arithmetic, comparisons, numeric casts, direct calls, loops, if, switch/br_table, phi copies; fma through host imports ash.fma_f64/fma_f32 for single rounding like the LLVM path; INT_MIN/-1 guarded because wasm traps where the reference wraps). Test ash_cli wasm_emit_numeric compiles a Haxe program and checks 40 results from the emitted module in wasmtime against the interpreter: all equal. Speed (macOS, load ~2, wasmtime, same functions, median of 5): fib(35) / loopSum(200M) / grid(400) = LLVM wasm default 168 / 531 / 188 ms; LLVM wasm with ASH_FIBER_POLLS=0 169 / 133 / 189; emitted, no polls or shadow frames 26 / 170 / 56; emitted with the shadow call stack and loop polls modelled as in-module wasm functions 56 / 405 / 76. Reading: loop polls cost LLVM about 4x on a tight loop (531 vs 133) and the emitter about 2.4x (405 vs 170); without polls LLVM is 28% faster than the emitter on loopSum (133 vs 170) - its one clear win. The LLVM module's fib is 3x slower than the emitter's even with the same shadow-frame protocol; the cause is not established. Not comparable beyond this: numeric kernels only, no objects/GC/strings/exceptions, polls modelled as a flag test, shadow stack simplified (no throw unwinding).","files":null}]}